WEBVTT

00:00:00.607 --> 00:00:22.271
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got salmon funding — a hundred and six million dollars announced with zero breakdown for where it actually lands — plus a Coast Salish Elder who's fought thirty years over one creek, one highway, one treaty. What makes a fight like that hold up when a five-year tech job folds overnight? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:38.246 --> 00:00:59.614
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Friday, August twenty-first, twenty twenty-six. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Wild Spaces &amp; Outdoor Life.

00:01:00.614 --> 00:01:33.822
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're talking trails, fire, and the tools people carry into places where nobody else can help them. Quick weather check — Horsefly Lake is sitting at fourteen degrees this morning with clear skies, high of thirty expected, low of twelve tonight, with light drizzle predicted for tomorrow. Around the region: 100 Mile House at fifteen, Williams Lake at fifteen, Quesnel at thirteen, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Anahim Lake is sitting at twelve, all clear. It's a hot one across the Cariboo — stay hydrated, and keep an eye on the BC Wildfire Dashboard if you're heading out.

00:01:34.022 --> 00:01:34.862
<v Riley>Let's get into it.

00:01:36.262 --> 00:02:02.110
<v Riley>Six stories close to home this morning before we even reach the theme. Starting in the South Cariboo: Barbara Hansen's regular column in the 100 Mile Free Press describes Lac La Hache settling back into ordinary rhythms after a wildfire scare pushed the community toward evacuation earlier this season. No new orders, crews scaled back, and residents who'd packed vehicles days earlier are unpacking them.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:19.622
<v Casey>Closer to Williams Lake, paving crews wrapped work this week on Johnson Street between Sixth and Western, and on Pigeon Avenue between Comer and Western. That closure ran from August 11th through the 20th, so if you've been detouring around that stretch, you should have your normal route back.

00:02:20.822 --> 00:02:47.462
<v Riley>Turning to provincial politics — the BC Conservatives have shuffled their party executive. Leader Kerry-Lynne Findlay has replaced party president Aisha Estey with Mauro Francis, a former federal Conservative candidate, and named a new vice-president alongside him. No policy shift has been announced, this is a personnel change inside the party structure, but it's the second shake-up under Findlay's leadership in recent months.

00:02:47.996 --> 00:02:51.132
<v Casey>Worth watching whether that pattern continues or settles.

00:02:52.332 --> 00:03:10.556
<v Riley>Ottawa and the province are putting up a hundred and six and a half million dollars combined to help recover collapsing Pacific salmon stocks. Federal Fisheries Minister Joanne Thompson made the announcement yesterday, framing it as a response to years of declining returns across multiple runs.

00:03:11.977 --> 00:03:18.553
<v Casey>That's a large number for a fishery that's been in freefall for the better part of a decade. Where does it actually land?

00:03:20.013 --> 00:03:43.197
<v Riley>The announcement doesn't break the funding down by watershed. It's framed at the provincial level — stock assessment, habitat restoration, hatchery support. Fraser River systems, which run straight through this region on their way to spawning grounds around Williams Lake and beyond, are central to why the total is that large, but there's no public line item yet for what happens locally.

00:03:44.679 --> 00:03:49.503
<v Casey>So the number is real, the accountability for where it goes isn't. We'll come back to that.

00:03:50.503 --> 00:04:14.959
<v Casey>Sticking with gear people actually pack for the outdoors — Live for the Outdoors ran a test this week where they left eight different cool boxes and cooler bags in a garden for twenty-four hours to see which held ice the longest. Nothing revolutionary, but if you're outfitting a truck for a lake weekend before summer ends, it's a useful buyer's guide, and the results varied more than you'd expect between similarly priced boxes.

00:04:15.474 --> 00:04:18.690
<v Riley>Function over marketing copy. Always the right instinct.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:45.770
<v Riley>Further afield, the Guardian is reporting that Porto Alegre, in southern Brazil, is bracing for another El Niño season two years after catastrophic flooding devastated the city. Reporting from the city's poorer backstreets describes residents saying officials never finished the recovery work from the last disaster, and now a new wet season is arriving on top of infrastructure that was never rebuilt to withstand the last one.

00:04:46.911 --> 00:04:54.071
<v Casey>"Recovery" and "rebuilt" aren't the same word, and the gap tends to fall hardest on the people with the least capacity to absorb it twice.

00:04:55.271 --> 00:05:21.319
<v Casey>Sticking with tools for staying connected off-grid — Meshtastic just released version 2.8 of its open-source mesh radio firmware. New features include AI-assisted message routing, location beacons, and NFC tag sharing between devices in the field. For anyone using these low-cost radios to stay in touch where cell towers don't reach, which describes most of the Chilcotin plateau, this is a real upgrade to a system a lot of backcountry users already run.

00:05:22.782 --> 00:05:30.798
<v Riley>These nodes run somewhere around fifty dollars each. That's a fraction of a satellite communicator subscription, and it doesn't require a monthly bill.

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:48.693
<v Casey>Hackaday covered a technique for pulling microplastics out of contaminated soil using tumbling magnetic flakes. Aquatic environments are relatively easy to filter, but soil traps particles at a scale that's been much harder to reach without excavating the ground. Lab-bench approach, not deployed yet.

00:05:49.893 --> 00:06:12.797
<v Riley>Flock Safety, the surveillance camera company that's drawn scrutiny from lawmakers on both sides of the aisle, announced new platform guardrails this week after a wave of cities cut ties over how police were accessing its footage. Multiple agencies had pulled out amid concerns the data was being used for immigration enforcement or unauthorized tracking outside its stated purpose.

00:06:14.067 --> 00:06:19.835
<v Casey>Guardrails announced after the backlash, not before it. That's the pattern with most of these platforms.

00:06:21.064 --> 00:06:37.528
<v Riley>And off the coast of Oman, salvage crews have begun work on a leaking tanker. Greenpeace estimates the spill has already spread across thirteen hundred square kilometres of open water, threatening marine ecosystems along a shipping corridor that sees heavy tanker traffic.

00:06:38.728 --> 00:07:03.376
<v Casey>Two more before we head into today's theme. Gizmodo has a story on whale calls that appeared, at first, to break the laws of physics — a hydrophone operator spent hours convinced his software had a bug. It turned out the receiver was picking up both the direct call and its reflected echo bouncing off the surface when a whale was near the top of the water column, which made a single call look like it arrived twice from two directions.

00:07:04.534 --> 00:07:11.014
<v Riley>Nature cheating the instruments, or the instruments not accounting for how sound actually moves underwater.

00:07:12.014 --> 00:07:36.326
<v Riley>And Resilience-dot-org looked at the mounting body of research suggesting fish feel pain — anatomical evidence, behavioural responses to analgesics, the kind of criteria that's convinced scientists other animals experience pain. It's a live debate with real consequences for how commercial and recreational fisheries get regulated, including the runs we were just talking about in the salmon story.

00:07:37.126 --> 00:07:51.486
<v Casey>Last one for the roundup. TechRadar reports scientists have developed a zinc-iodine battery that can handle more than sixty thousand charge cycles, fully charges in about three minutes, and doesn't carry the fire risk that comes with lithium chemistries.

00:07:52.678 --> 00:08:01.286
<v Riley>Sixty thousand cycles against a lithium pack in an electric vehicle, which is lucky to hold up for two thousand before it degrades meaningfully.

00:08:02.488 --> 00:08:17.952
<v Casey>If that chemistry scales past a lab bench, that's every cordless tool, every remote weather station, every backcountry sensor running longer and charging faster without the fire hazard. It's early, but it's a number to keep an eye on as it moves toward commercial production.

00:08:19.223 --> 00:08:27.287
<v Riley>We'll believe it when it ships. On that note, let's talk about an organization that runs on volunteer hours rather than battery chemistry.

00:08:28.687 --> 00:08:58.415
<v Casey>The Scout Island Nature Centre in Williams Lake is a volunteer-run education centre operated by the Williams Lake Field Naturalists, tucked right into the marsh at the edge of the lake. Right now they're deep into their summer kids' programs, and every week runs a different theme — Water one week, Bugs the next, Birds after that. They also run Salmon in the Classroom, which puts local kids in direct contact with the fish we were just talking about, raising eggs and tracking their development before release. Sign up at scoutisland dot ca.

00:08:59.004 --> 00:09:09.620
<v Riley>Volunteer-run and still going every summer without a press release attached — that's the kind of infrastructure that never shows up in a funding announcement but keeps working anyway.

00:09:09.973 --> 00:09:11.477
<v Casey>Let's get into today's Deep Dive.

00:09:12.877 --> 00:09:26.605
<v Riley>There's a Coast Salish Elder named ZȺWIZUT, Carl Olsen, who's spent thirty years fighting to protect salmon habitat around a W̱SÁNEĆ village site from a highway expansion. Thirty years, same creek, same fight, and it isn't over yet.

00:09:27.505 --> 00:09:29.801
<v Casey>Thirty years is a long time to hold one line.

00:09:30.398 --> 00:09:48.526
<v Riley>It started when he was arrested for hunting on land the Crown said he had no right to use. That arrest turned into a landmark court case, and he's been holding the Crown to the terms of the Douglas Treaties ever since — the same treaties that protect fishing and habitat access along that stretch of coastline.

00:09:49.069 --> 00:09:57.557
<v Casey>And he's still fighting the same fight three decades later. That's either remarkable persistence or a sign the system was never built to resolve these claims quickly.

00:09:58.112 --> 00:10:12.528
<v Riley>Probably both. It puts a real question on the table: what's the difference between someone who spent five years building a product nobody used, gave up, and walked away with nothing, and someone who's spent thirty years on one piece of ground?

00:10:12.865 --> 00:10:22.753
<v Casey>One of them has something concrete to point to. A creek. A run of fish. A community that still depends on the outcome. The other has a resume line and, this year, probably a layoff.

00:10:23.371 --> 00:10:45.323
<v Riley>The legal theory ZȺWIZUT is using has precedent with teeth. In Washington State, the U.S. Supreme Court deadlocked four to four in 2018 on a treaty case rooted in the Stevens Treaties. That deadlock let stand a lower court ruling that forced the state to replace hundreds of culverts blocking salmon migration. The estimated cost ran into the billions.

00:10:45.823 --> 00:10:50.095
<v Casey>Billions of dollars, and how many years of litigation did it take to get there?

00:10:50.371 --> 00:10:58.067
<v Riley>Decades. That litigation traces back to the 1970s. Two generations of lawyers and biologists to force the state's hand.

00:10:58.581 --> 00:11:13.533
<v Casey>So the win is real, but it arrives after the damage is done. Nobody stopped a highway or a culvert before it went in. The court forced a fix decades later, after entire runs had already collapsed. That's a very slow repair job with a court order attached, not prevention.

00:11:14.195 --> 00:11:22.795
<v Riley>It's still a repair that happened. Most infrastructure damage to habitat just stays broken, because nobody with legal standing ever forces the question.

00:11:23.411 --> 00:11:51.219
<v Casey>Sure. But ZȺWIZUT isn't operating from a position of institutional patience. He watched one legal framework take thirty years to move an inch, and he isn't willing to wait for the next round of litigation before this highway expansion happens. Watershed Sentinel documented a parallel case at Goldstream, where courts recognized W̱SÁNEĆ treaty rights to hunt and fish "as formerly" and protect shellfish-harvesting grounds — and even that recognition didn't stop new development pressure from showing up again a few years later.

00:11:51.871 --> 00:11:59.783
<v Riley>Which tells you the win doesn't lock in permanently either. It has to be defended again every time someone new proposes a project.

00:12:00.321 --> 00:12:10.713
<v Casey>That's the part that should temper any optimism about courts eventually forcing the right outcome. They force one outcome, once, on one file. The next highway needs its own thirty-year fight.

00:12:11.381 --> 00:12:41.381
<v Riley>That tension maps onto something underneath a lot of the tech burnout writing that's been circulating this week. A knowledge worker puts five years into something, the mission-driven narrative collapses the moment the funding does, and they walk away with nothing to show for it — no product, no community depending on the outcome. ZȺWIZUT has thirty years and an unfinished fight, but he has a creek. He has fish. He has something the work was actually about, whether or not the legal battle ever fully resolves.

00:12:41.481 --> 00:12:50.569
<v Riley>The land-based fight doesn't get easier by being real. It doesn't get hollow the same way a mission statement does when the company deletes it from the website.

00:12:51.147 --> 00:13:09.443
<v Casey>I'd push back on "doesn't get hollow." Ask anyone who's spent three decades on one legal file whether every single day felt purposeful, or whether a lot of it felt like grinding against a system built to outlast the person challenging it. Exhaustion and purpose aren't opposites. You can have both at once, for thirty years.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:21.104
<v Riley>No argument there. But exhaustion with a target is different from exhaustion with no target at all. That's the gap between ZȺWIZUT's thirty years and a laid-off product manager's five.

00:13:21.703 --> 00:13:25.735
<v Casey>Sure. What does any of that have to do with people hiking a trail in New Hampshire?

00:13:26.014 --> 00:13:57.718
<v Riley>There's a piece running this week built around a book called "Up on Cove Mountain." A journalist named Earl Swift is revisiting the 1990 murders of two hikers, Molly LaRue and Geoff Hood, on the Appalachian Trail — a couple he'd crossed paths with weeks before they were killed. What stands out in the actual reporting is how rare that kind of event was. Multiple sources put it at roughly five killings across four incidents on that trail in the decades before 1990, against millions of people who've hiked sections of it.

00:13:58.237 --> 00:14:04.141
<v Casey>And yet it's the story that gets a book deal thirty-some years later, not the millions of ordinary, uneventful hikes.

00:14:04.788 --> 00:14:25.476
<v Riley>Hikers and trail community members quoted in the coverage still say the Appalachian Trail is one of the safest places they've spent time. The true-crime framing distorts the actual risk calculation. Weather, falls, and medical emergencies are the real hazards on that trail, by a wide margin, and none of them make for a compelling narrative.

00:14:26.152 --> 00:14:52.728
<v Casey>That surface read undersells what Swift is actually doing. He's not going back to recalculate odds. He's going back because he can't reconcile how thin the line was between his trip and theirs. That's a psychological reckoning with randomness, and it lands differently depending on who's hearing it. A solo woman deciding whether to hike alone doesn't experience that story as an outlier data point. She experiences it as a live possibility, whether the numbers back that up or not.

00:14:53.247 --> 00:15:18.911
<v Riley>Which connects to something specific about who tech burnout is sending outdoors right now. A lot of people leaving the industry describe wanting something real — dirt, distance, physical risk, a body that gets tired instead of a mind that's just anxious. But wild spaces come with their own actual dangers, rare as the violent ones are, and their own randomness that has nothing to do with anyone's career crisis.

00:15:19.451 --> 00:15:29.467
<v Casey>So trading a hollow office job for a long hike doesn't hand you purpose automatically. It hands you a different set of risks, some serious, and calls the whole thing authenticity.

00:15:30.112 --> 00:15:36.160
<v Riley>(wry) Roughly that. The land doesn't owe anyone meaning just because it's harder than a Slack channel.

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:57.820
<v Casey>Which loops back to ZȺWIZUT. His fight isn't meaningful because it happens outdoors. It's meaningful because there's a specific, accountable relationship attached to it — a treaty, a creek, a community whose fish run depends on the outcome. A solo thru-hike isn't a structure. It's an experience, a valuable one, but it doesn't come with anyone else depending on how it turns out.

00:15:58.382 --> 00:16:14.998
<v Riley>Agreed, and that's the piece the tech burnout conversation keeps missing. It isn't land versus office as categories. It's whether the work is tied to an outcome someone else depends on, with accountability that outlasts a single funding cycle or a founder's attention span.

00:16:15.898 --> 00:16:43.810
<v Casey>Which is where the salmon funding announcement bites. A hundred and six and a half million dollars for stock recovery sounds impressive in a press release. It also sounds like exactly the kind of number that funds a pilot program, generates a good headline, and quietly dies once the initial grant cycle ends. The Fraser runs straight through here. Are Cariboo communities and Nations getting a say in where that money lands, or is the map being drawn in Ottawa and Victoria and stopping somewhere south of us?

00:16:44.126 --> 00:17:08.238
<v Riley>The announcement doesn't name a Cariboo-specific allocation, and that's the gap worth watching over the next year. If Fraser sockeye and chinook stocks are central to why that total is as large as it is, spawning grounds around Xatśūll territory and the stretches near Williams Lake First Nation should be part of where the money lands — not treated as a downstream afterthought to projects closer to the river's mouth.

00:17:08.895 --> 00:17:21.871
<v Casey>And who's tracking whether that happens? A press release from a federal minister isn't an accountability mechanism, it's a photo opportunity with a dollar figure attached. Somebody local needs to be watching the actual disbursement schedule.

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:35.628
<v Riley>That's a concrete ask a regional district or a Nation's fisheries office could put forward right now — request a public accounting of where the funds land, broken out by watershed, not just a provincial total that never gets itemized.

00:17:36.128 --> 00:17:49.352
<v Casey>Compare that appetite for line-item accountability to what happens with a laid-off tech worker's severance. Nobody is asking a Silicon Valley company to file a public accounting of what happened to its mission statement after the funding round collapsed.

00:17:49.252 --> 00:17:50.196
<v Riley>Ha. No, they're not.

00:17:50.824 --> 00:18:09.768
<v Casey>The trades and the land-based work aren't happier because they happen outdoors. They're harder to walk away from empty-handed, because the accountability is built into the relationship itself — a treaty that predates the company, a watershed that outlasts the funding cycle, a Nation that's still there next year no matter who's in charge of the budget.

00:18:10.384 --> 00:18:15.512
<v Riley>Whereas a mission statement gets deleted from the website overnight, and nobody notices.

00:18:16.118 --> 00:18:30.174
<v Casey>So maybe outdoor work isn't inherently more meaningful than knowledge work — it's structurally harder to abandon, and that structure is what people leaving tech are actually hungry for, even when they describe the craving as wanting to get outside more.

00:18:30.719 --> 00:18:45.807
<v Riley>That much tracks. What's still open is whether that kind of structure can be built on purpose, or whether it only exists where a treaty or a Nation's authority already forces it into being. You can't legislate thirty years of commitment into a startup.

00:18:46.475 --> 00:19:09.827
<v Casey>You could put a smaller version of the same test to something local right now, though. Ask whether any Cariboo watershed board or regional fisheries office publishes a public ledger of where provincial and federal recovery dollars actually go, year over year, broken down by project. If none of them do, that's worth pushing before the next funding announcement arrives with another large number and no map attached.

00:19:10.448 --> 00:19:26.792
<v Riley>That doesn't need a grant application to answer. It needs a phone call to a fisheries office and someone willing to ask an uncomfortable follow-up question when the first answer is vague. If you've got that phone number, send it our way — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:19:27.311 --> 00:19:33.207
<v Casey>And if you're the one who makes that call, tell us what they say. We'd like to know if the ledger exists.

00:19:33.552 --> 00:19:36.632
<v Riley>(warmly) Enjoy the weekend, and get outside while the weather holds.

00:19:36.965 --> 00:19:39.181
<v Casey>I'll settle for the ledger. See you Monday.